.SH NAME
hed\-shell \- :shell runs an external command.
With no arguments it opens a !
prompt; with arguments it runs them directly.
Both paths share the same execution: \[en]skipwait flag, %\-token
expansion against the current buffer, and trailing capture tokens that
splice stdout back into the buffer.
.PP
\f[CR]:shell\f[R] runs an external command.
With no arguments it opens a \f[CR]!\f[R] prompt; with arguments it runs
them directly.
Both paths share the same execution: \f[CR]\-\-skipwait\f[R] flag,
\f[CR]%\f[R]\-token expansion against the current buffer, and trailing
capture tokens that splice stdout back into the buffer.
.SH Usage
.IP
.EX
:shell <cmd>           # run <cmd> in a child shell, then wait for Enter
:shell                 # open the \[ga]!\[ga] prompt; submit runs the line
:shell \-\-skipwait <cmd> # don\[aq]t wait for Enter when the command finishes
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]\-\-skipwait\f[R] flag is recognized anywhere in the command
line as long as it stands alone (whitespace\-separated).
.SH %\-token expansion
\f[CR]%\f[R]\-tokens are replaced with shell\-escaped values from the
current buffer.
They\[cq]re recognized only at word boundaries (the next char must be
non\-letter or end\-of\-string), so \f[CR]%path\f[R] stays literal.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
