T}
T{
\f[CR]>%v\f[R]
T}@T{
replace the visual selection with stdout (charwise / linewise; block not
supported)
T}
T{
\f[CR]>%y\f[R]
T}@T{
store stdout in the unnamed yank register, no buffer edit
T}
.TE
.PP
Examples:
.IP
.EX
:shell sort \-u %b >%b      # sort & dedupe the buffer in place
:shell date >>%b           # insert today\[aq]s date at the cursor
:shell jq . >%v            # pretty\-print the selected JSON
:shell git rev\-parse HEAD >%y  # yank the current commit
.EE
.PP
The capture path bypasses the interactive child\-process flow \[em] no
\f[CR]\-\-skipwait\f[R] wait, no terminal handoff.
Stderr is \f[I]not\f[R] captured; non\-zero exit codes still produce
whatever stdout was written.
.SH The \f[CR]!\f[R] prompt
\f[CR]:shell\f[R] with no args opens a one\-line prompt labelled
\f[CR]!\f[R].
Submit runs the typed line through the same \f[CR]shell_execute\f[R]
path, so \f[CR]%\f[R]\-tokens, \f[CR]\-\-skipwait\f[R], and capture
tokens all work there too.
Cancel (\f[CR]<Esc>\f[R]) does nothing.
.SH Public API
.IP
.EX
#include \f[B]\[dq]shell/shell.h\[dq]\f[R]

void cmd_shell(const char *args);
.EE
.PP
Exported so other plugins can shell out without re\-parsing through the
colon\-command machinery \[em] the \f[CR]treesitter\f[R] plugin uses it
to run \f[CR]:tsi\f[R] installer commands.
